{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially during downtrends (typical overestimate ~1–4 EUR, growing to ~5–12 EUR at the 3–4 week horizon in sharp selloffs). Apply a downward bias correction that increases with forecast horizon.",
    "Downtrend lag/underreaction: when recent history shows a clear decline, the model decays too slowly and predicts stabilization/rebound too early. Add extra negative drift proportional to recent slope (e.g., last 5–10 day trend) to reduce lag.",
    "Rebound overshoot: after short-term bounces in the forecast path, the model tends to overshoot and hold levels too high; realized series mean-reverts lower. Damp forecast rebounds (shrink positive day-to-day changes) and enforce stronger mean reversion toward the recent lower range.",
    "Horizon-specific error structure: near-term (days 1–7) errors are modest but still upward; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially upward. Use piecewise calibration: small downward shift short-term, larger downward shift plus added negative drift beyond ~day 10.",
    "Tail-risk underestimation on selloffs: model fails to capture the depth of late-horizon drawdowns (realized breaks to new lows while forecast stays elevated). Introduce a conditional ‘selloff regime’ adjustment: if recent volatility + negative trend exceed a threshold, apply an additional step-down and lower terminal level."
  ]
}
